        "id": "ch15_5_2-2",
        "text": "I -PullMode , which may be set to Off , PullDown , or PullUp , and may be needed with\nsome chipsets, but not the Pi. The default value is Null .\nI A -Raw switch, which returns a value of High or Low .\n\u2022 Set-GpioPin : This cmdlet sets the voltage on a specified pin to High or Low . It has\nthree parameters:\nI -Id , which takes an Int32 value and specifies the pin.\nI -Value , which takes either High or Low .\nI -PassThru , which, by default, results in the cmdlet returning nothing. If we want it to\nreturn a PowerShell object confirming that the value was set, then we can use this parameter.\n\u2022 Get-I2CDevice : This cmdlet creates an I2C device object with a friendly name that we can\nthen use with the *-I2Cregister cmdlets. It has two parameters:\nI -Id , which takes an Int32 value and specifies the address of the device\nI -FriendlyName , which we use to assign a string to the device\n\u2022 Get-I2Cregister : This cmdlet gets the value held in a register on a particular device.\nThere are four parameters:\nI -Device , which takes an I2C device object\nI -Register , which takes a Uint16 value that specifies the register on the device we want\nto read\nI - R a w , which returns the value stored in the register, rather than an\nI2CdeviceRegisterData object\nI -Bytecount , which takes a byte value and specifies the number of bytes expected in the data\nSimple physical computing\n379\n\u2022 Set-I2Cregister : This cmdlet sets the register value on a device. There are four parameters:\nI -Device , which takes an I2C device object.\nI -Register , which takes a Uint16 value that specifies the register on the device we\nwant to set.\nI -Data , a value in bytes to be written to the register.\nI -PassThru , which, by default, results in the cmdlet returning nothing. If we want it to\nreturn a PowerShell object confirming that the value was set, then we can use this parameter.",
